meta {
  name: Update and Delete Channel
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

delete {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/channels/REPLACE_WITH_CHANNEL_ID
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-15 against a fresh disposable scratch channel
  ("WC Test Channel For Patch Delete", format `CSV`, id
  `6aa982cba30ca5945169a253`, created and destroyed in this same run) on
  "David's Dev Account" -- retested from scratch since the original
  version of this file (named "... (Undeclared)") pointed at a throwaway
  id from 2026-09-08 with no `example{}`. `openapi_pimv3.json`'s
  2026-09-15 refresh now formally declares `PATCH`/`DELETE` for
  `/channels/{identifier}` (previously undeclared but working, quirk
  #20/#45).

  ## What it does
  - `PATCH /channels/{id}` with `{"name": "..."}` -- `200`, renames the
    channel. Full channel shape returned (same rich default shape as
    create, quirk #41) -- confirms two new fields not previously
    surfaced in this project's Phase 4 create-response spot-check:
    `is_fmt` (`false`) and `rebuild_once`/`rebuild_once_at`/
    `rebuild_once_timezone` (all `null`/`false`), alongside the
    already-documented `rebuild_periodically` family (quirk #40).
  - `DELETE /channels/{id}` -- `204`, then `404` on a follow-up `GET`.
}

example {
  name: 200 - Success
  description: Real response captured 2026-09-15 against a disposable scratch channel on "David's Dev Account".

  request: {
    url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/channels/6aa982cba30ca5945169a253
    method: patch
    mode: json
    headers: {
      Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
      Content-Type: application/json
      Accept: application/json
    }
    body:json: {
      {
        "name": "WC Test Channel Renamed"
      }
    }
  }

